    content: 'Bitcoin privacy is the practice of limiting how easily observers can connect transactions, addresses, purchases, identities, locations, and real-world behavior. It includes both on-chain privacy and off-chain privacy around identity, vendors, devices, communication, support tools, and cloud systems. Key points: Bitcoin\'s ledger is public, so transaction privacy requires deliberate practices. Off-chain data such as KYC records, delivery addresses, invoices, and support tickets can be more identifying than on-chain data. Privacy tools are only one part of the problem; behavior and data minimization matter too. Privacy can be a safety issue, not just a preference. Bitcoin is pseudonymous, not anonymous by default. Good privacy is not absolute invisibility: it is often a practical reduction of linkability, exposure and unnecessary data collection. Why it matters: When ownership data becomes linkable to a real person, Bitcoin holders can face scams, surveillance, extortion, or physical attacks.',
