    level: 'beginner',
    content: 'Financial censorship occurs when a state, banking, payment or platform actor blocks, freezes, limits, punitively monitors or conditions access to money and payments. Key points: It can take the form of frozen accounts, capital controls, withdrawal limits, debanking, payment refusals, caps, geofencing or filtering of certain activities. The course cites Greece in 2015 as an example of banking constraint in a crisis. Financial censorship can be visible in a crisis, but also quiet in daily life through scoring, compliance, account closures or denied access. Bitcoin reduces dependence on intermediaries when used in self-custody; stablecoins can reduce some frictions but generally remain freezable or censorable. A CBDC can increase the capacity to filter, slow down or condition payments if the architecture and the law allow it. Censorship is often invisible until the crisis. Why it matters: It is one of the best angles for explaining Bitcoin sovereignty, the importance of cash, the limits of centralised stablecoins and the political risks of CBDCs.',
