    level: 'beginner',
    content: 'Financial surveillance is the set of obligations, tools and infrastructure that turn payments, accounts, identities and monetary flows into data that intermediaries and authorities can consult, correlate or exploit. In the Bitcoin and crypto context, it crystallises around KYC/AML, on-chain traceability, sanctions, mixers, self-hosted wallets, reporting obligations and, potentially, CBDCs. Key points: AML/CFT enforcement can conflict with privacy, the presumption of innocence and technological neutrality. Privacy tools are often targeted because they expose the limits of the surveillance-by-default model. KYC databases also create private risks: data leaks, targeting, blackmail, debanking. The Tornado Cash and Samourai Wallet cases serve as case studies on the liability of developers and operators. With AMLR/TFR, the concrete stake is the link between civil identity, bitcoin address and transaction history, which can produce lasting surveillance if it becomes systematic. A CBDC can extend this logic by making money itself more visible, more programmable and easier to condition. Why it matters: A pivotal topic connecting Bitcoin privacy, regulation, KYC, physical security, no-KYC finance, CBDCs and civil liberties.',
