    level: 'intermediate',
    content: 'A BGP Hijacking attack manipulates internet routing to redirect traffic between Bitcoin nodes or miners, enabling isolation, surveillance, or hash power theft. Key points: BGP (Border Gateway Protocol) governs routing between autonomous systems (AS) on the internet. An attacker advertising false IP ownership can intercept or block Bitcoin node-to-node traffic. Mining pools are a specific target: redirecting Stratum traffic diverts hash power to the attacker\'s pool. Since ASICs rarely restart, such a redirection can persist for extended periods. The attack can aim at censorship, double-spend facilitation, or mining revenue theft. ASMap (BIP) partially mitigates this by diversifying peer connections across autonomous systems. Why it matters: BGP Hijacking is a realistic network-level attack vector on Bitcoin infrastructure. It exploits BGP\'s trust-by-design weakness rather than Bitcoin\'s cryptographic layer.',
